const body = `<h1>Reading Files: File + Scanner</h1>

<p>
    CED topic 4.6, new in the 2025 revision. The idea is small and mighty: the
    Scanner you already know can wrap a File instead of System.in, and suddenly
    your programs eat data instead of keyboard taps. The JQR lists exactly what
    you need: File's constructor, Scanner's File constructor, six next-methods,
    hasNext, and close.
</p>

<h2>The Minimal Loop</h2>

<pre><code>import java.util.Scanner;
import java.io.File;

Scanner in = new Scanner(new File("monsters.txt"));
while (in.hasNext()) {
    String line = in.nextLine();
    // process line
}
in.close();</code></pre>

<p>Four lines of ceremony, one loop, and the file is yours. The shape
"while hasNext, do next" is called the <strong>ask-permission pattern</strong>,
and it is the only file loop you need for line-based data.</p>

<h2>The JQR Methods, All Nine</h2>

<table>
    <tr><th>Method</th><th>Returns</th><th>Use it when</th></tr>
    <tr><td><code>new File("name")</code></td><td>a File handle</td><td>the door. Does not open or read anything by itself</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>new Scanner(file)</code></td><td>a Scanner aimed at the file</td><td>attaching the reader</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>hasNext()</code></td><td>boolean</td><td>the loop guard. "Is there anything left?"</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>nextLine()</code></td><td>String (the whole line)</td><td>line-based data: records, maps, save files. THE default.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>next()</code></td><td>String (one token, no spaces)</td><td>space-free data. Treats spaces as separators. Handle with care.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>nextInt()</code></td><td>int</td><td>whitespace-separated numbers. No parsing needed.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>nextDouble()</code></td><td>double</td><td>same, for decimals</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>nextBoolean()</code></td><td>boolean</td><td>reads "true"/"false" tokens</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>close()</code></td><td>void</td><td>when done. Always. Two-pass reading (8.6) NEEDS it.</td></tr>
</table>

<div class="callout trap">
    <strong>nextLine vs next and the mixed-call trap.</strong> The JQR itself
    warns: nextLine "can return the empty string if called immediately after
    another Scanner method." Because next/nextInt stop BEFORE their trailing
    newline, and nextLine reads up TO the next newline, mixing them leaves an
    invisible empty line. Rule of thumb: pick one style per Scanner. Line-based
    files (records, maps, saves): nextLine + split. Pure number streams:
    nextInt. Do not mix unless you enjoy debugging invisible strings.
</div>

<h2>The Exception Story</h2>

<pre><code>public static void main(String[] args) throws FileNotFoundException { ... }</code></pre>

<p>The compiler demands <code>throws FileNotFoundException</code> because
<code>new Scanner(new File(...))</code> can fail: the file might not exist.
Adding the clause is you signing the release form. If the file is actually
missing at runtime, the program dies with a stack trace whose first useful line
names the missing file and the line of your code that asked for it. Reading
that trace calmly is a skill, and 7.6a trains it by making you cause the error
on purpose.</p>

<h2>The Standard Loader, Complete</h2>

<pre><code>public static ArrayList&lt;Monster&gt; loadMonsters(String filename)
        throws FileNotFoundException {

    ArrayList&lt;Monster&gt; bestiary = new ArrayList&lt;Monster&gt;();
    Scanner in = new Scanner(new File(filename));

    while (in.hasNext()) {
        String line = in.nextLine().trim();
        if (line.isEmpty()) continue;              // skip blank lines

        String[] f = line.split(" ");
        if (f.length != 5) {                        // guard: shape
            System.out.println("skipping bad line: " + line);
            continue;
        }
        try {
            Monster m = new Monster(f[0],
                Integer.parseInt(f[1]),
                Integer.parseInt(f[2]),
                Integer.parseInt(f[3]));
            bestiary.add(m);
        } catch (NumberFormatException e) {
            System.out.println("skipping bad numbers: " + line);
        }
    }
    in.close();
    return bestiary;
}</code></pre>

<p>Every piece is a lesson you already own: the loop (this page), split and
parseInt (2.9a), guards (6.6b), objects (Unit 5), ArrayList (7.1). File reading
is not a new topic. It is the reunion episode.</p>

<h2>Saving: The Other Direction</h2>

<p>The CED only requires READING files. Writing is out of scope, which is why
this course cheats elegantly with terminal redirection:</p>

<pre><code>$ java Game &gt; save.txt     # everything printed lands in save.txt</code></pre>

<p>Your game already prints beautifully. Redirection makes printing into
saving. It is a shell feature, not a Java feature, and it is enough to build a
full save/load cycle in 7.6b.</p>

<h2>Tinker Challenge: Two Loaders</h2>

<div class="tinker">
    <h3>Same File, Two Ways</h3>
    <p>Load <a href="../../data/monsters.txt">monsters.txt</a> twice:</p>
    <ol>
        <li>With nextLine + split (the standard pattern).</li>
        <li>With next and nextInt directly, no split, no parsing. Name with
        next(), stats with nextInt().</li>
    </ol>
    <p>The second version is shorter. So why is it the fragile one? Break it:
        add a monster with a two-word name ("cave troll") and run both loaders.
        The next-based version reads "cave" as the name and "troll" as the hp
        and crashes on parseInt... or worse, silently misaligns every field
        after it. Line-based loading survives surprises; token-based loading
        assumes perfection. Write the rule in your notes.</p>
</div>

<h2>Socratic Checkpoint</h2>

<div class="socratic">
    <h3>Why is file I/O in the curriculum now when it wasn't before?</h3>
    <p>Because data analysis without data is a chalkboard exercise. The revised
    CED chains 4.2 (datasets) into 4.6 (files) into 4.8-4.10 (ArrayList
    algorithms): real records, from real files, into real structures, answered
    with real algorithms. Each topic justifies the next. Reading a file is the
    door from "toy programs" to "programs that touch the world."</p>
    <h3>Why close()?</h3>
    <p>Two reasons. Practical: the OS tracks open files, and carelessness leaks
    resources (8.6's two-pass read FAILS without close between passes).
    Ethical: if the file is on shared storage, an open handle can lock out
    other programs. Closing is the cleanup chore that professional code always
    does, like a chef cleaning the station.</p>
</div>

